// @reject: loads export_block_at_file_level_m.zy, whose export block sits at file
// @reject: top level instead of inside a `# name { … }` module block. The module
// @reject: is the error, so importing it is an error too, wherever it is imported
// @reject: from — the same shape as 02_computed_module_initializer.zy.
// @reject: Both Rust engines: `failed to parse module: 2 parse error(s)`.
// @reject: The browser engine prints 14.
// @reject: NOTE — the imported file deliberately carries NO numeric prefix, and
// @reject: renaming it to `03_…_m.zy` for tidiness would silently break this test.
// @reject: A module path whose last component starts with a digit fails to lex in
// @reject: every engine (`expected module path` in Rust, `Expected IDENT, got '0'`
// @reject: in JS), so such a file is refused for the path and never reaches the
// @reject: rule under test. 02 and 04 had that defect — found 2026-08-31 and
// @reject: written down HERE, in the file that got it right, where it sat
// @reject: unacted-on. Their modules were renamed 2026-09-19 (step 3.7): 02 now
// @reject: reaches E013 and 04 reaches E014, in all three engines. The rule
// @reject: stands: an imported module file takes no numeric prefix.
<# ./export_block_at_file_level_m => m
>> m::publica() ¶
